   "cls": "info",
   "msg": "Shellcode is raw machine code injected into a vulnerable buffer. The challenge: most injection vectors (strcpy, gets) stop at null bytes (0x00). Your shellcode must be null-free.",
   "srcLine": null,
   "done": false
  },
  {
   "cls": "info",
   "msg": "Trick 1: XOR-zeroing. mov eax, 0 encodes as B8 00 00 00 00 (4 null bytes!). Instead: xor eax, eax encodes as 31 C0 — same result, no nulls.",
   "srcLine": 9,
   "done": false
  },
  {
   "cls": "info",
   "msg": "Trick 2: Partial register writes. mov eax, 0x0b has nulls. But xor eax, eax; mov al, 0x0b — XOR to zero, then write only the low byte. Two instructions, no nulls.",
   "srcLine": 10,
   "done": false
  },
  {
   "cls": "info",
   "msg": "Trick 3: Stack string. Push the string in reverse, dword by dword. \"/bin//sh\" (note the double slash — Linux ignores it, avoids a 7-byte string that needs padding). Push null terminator first (via push eax after xor), then \"//sh\" (0x68732f2f), then \"/bin\" (0x6e69622f). mov ebx, esp gives you a pointer to the string.",
   "srcLine": 15,
   "done": false
  },
